{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011Ce4mZY8gVik828WvTQEj3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Answer\n\nI would add the new rule to **Section 2. Flag lifecycle**, immediately after the first bullet point:\n\n```markdown\n## 2. Flag lifecycle\n\n- **No flag outlives its purpose.** Each flag exists from the change\n  that introduces it until the day no path in the source can still\n  reach it; the steward listed with it carries it over that whole\n  span.\n- A flag is deleted once the rollout has stood at 100% for a full\n  release, not merely marked done — one left behind keeps both\n  branches running. Record the clearing release beside the figure.\n- **Rollout figures are stated, never inherited.** No flag takes a\n  default exposure; the flag sheet states the percentage and the\n  cohort it applies to.\n```\n\n## Rationale\n\nThis rule specifies the concrete mechanism for how flags reach their end-of-life (the deletion condition), which directly operationalizes the principle in the first bullet. Placing it immediately after clarifies the lifecycle sequence: flags live until they no longer serve their purpose (first bullet) — and here's exactly when and how we delete them (new rule) — and separately, how we track them while active (existing second bullet)."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":352,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":271,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}
